\begin_layout Title
Public Economics I: Class Discussion Notes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Does Aid Matter? Measuring the Effect of Student Aid on College Attendance
 and Completion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The author is interested in the effect of aid on a person's educational
 attainment.
 This relationship can be expressed with the following reduced-form equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
S_{i}=\alpha+\beta AID_{i}+\chi X_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{i}$
\end_inset

 is some measure of an individual's schooling, such as college attendance
 or completed schooling, 
\begin_inset Formula $AID_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the amount of student aid for which subject 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is eligible, and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a vector of individual covariates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In order to obtain an unbiased estimate of the causal effect of aid, the
 analyst must include in the regression all of the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 that affect both schooling and aid eligibility.
 Unfortunately, many of these covariates are typically unavailable to the
 econometrician.
 A discrete shift in the rules governing aid can induce variation that is
 uncorrelated with these unobserved determinants of schooling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The author uses a difference-in-difference methodolgy to examine the effect
 of eligibility for Social Security student benefits on college attendance
 and completed schooling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The key estimating equation is the following:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=\alpha+\beta(\mbox{Father Deceased}_{i}\times\mbox{Before}_{i})+\delta\mbox{Father Deceased}_{i}+\theta\mbox{Before}_{i}+v_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the dependent variable is a measure of college attendance or completion
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Before}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable that is set to one if a youth is a memeber of a cohort
 that graduated from high school before student benefits were eliminated.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Father Deceased}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable set to one for those who, due to the death of their
 father, were potentially eligible for child survivor benefits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The reduced-form effect of Social Security student benefits is captured
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 The specification controls for changes over time in average college attendance
 rates and average differences in the college attendance of those with a
 deceased father and those with a living father.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The key identifying assumption is that any relative shift in the attendance
 of the children of deceased fathers is attributable to the withdrawal of
 student benefits.
 To say the assumption in a different way, the change in enrollment rate
 in the treatment group would have been the same as the change in the control
 group in the absence of treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 captures the effect on schooling decisions of aid 
\emph on
eligibility
\emph default
 rather than aid 
\emph on
receipt
\emph default
.
 In the language of experimentalist literature, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 captures the effect of the 
\emph on
intention to treat
\emph default
.
 Since policy makers control the offer of aid, but not its take-up, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the parameter of interest if we wish to predict the effect of altering
 aid policy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
During the period under analysis, there is no statistically significant
 change in the differences in background characteristics between children
 with living and dead fathers.
 Table 1 therefore provides suggestive evidence that the additivity assumption
 of difference-in-differences holds for this analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The author uses OLS to estimate equation (2).
 Standard errors in the tables are corrected for within-household correlation
 in error terms due to the presence of siblings in the data, as well as
 for heteroskedasticity due to the dichotomous dependent variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Does drinking impair college performance? Evidence from a regression discontinui
ty approach.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors are estimating the effect drinking alcohol has on academic performan
ce.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The naive approach entails a simple cross-sectional analysis.
 A variable corresponding to academic performance is placed on the left-hand
 side, a variable measuring an amount a person drinks is on the right-hand
 side, and then a standard OLS regression is conducted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There are many problems with the cross-sectional method.
 A reverse causation story, i.e., poor academic performance causes people
 to drink more, is entirely plausible, and it is reasonable to assume that
 people who drink alcohol are less likely to study.
 Both of these stories would overstate the negative effect drinking has
 on academic performance.
 On the other hand, it is also possible to tell a story which leads to an
 understatement of the negative effect.
 For example, students who do well in school celebrate during their free
 time by drinking alcohol.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Why is determining the causal relationship between drinking alcohol and
 academic performance important? A labor economist would argue that college
 is an important experience in which young adults attain valuable human
 capital for their future employment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In order to obtain proper estimates, the others need to overcome the endogeneity
 issues.
 They do this by utilizing a regression discontinuity desig in which the
 running variable is date of birth.
 The authors compare students who turn 21 after final exams (the treatment
 group) with students who turn 21 before the final exams (control group).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The identifying assumption is the following: all other determinants of outcome
 are smooth across the cutoff.
 Why does this assumption hold? There is no manipulation of the running
 variable across the cutoff ex-ante because students are unable to manipulate
 their official age (i.e., date of birth).
 Also, no other determinants are changing across the age cutoff, i.e., there
 are no other discontinuities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 4 is comparing means.
 Note that the smaller the bandwidth, the more susceptible the slope of
 the line of best fit is to the noise of the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Working During School and Academic Performance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Important Rule: Always show the reduced-form regression.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 1 shows the results of the OLS regression from a naive cross-sectional
 design.
 Note that the estimates are biased.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Even though the paper uses an IV estimation methods, the authors could have
 also applied a differences-in-differences approach:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\alpha X_{i}+\beta P_{t}+\gamma_{i}+\theta H_{it}+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 2 provides descriptive statistics by job assignment.
 The authors use initial job assignment as an IV for hours worked.
 They need to show that initial job assignment was as-good-as random.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 3 gives the OLS regressions for first-semester hours and first-semester
 grades.
 The first column shows that first-semester hours are uncorrelated with
 other observable characteristics of individuals.
 The second column is the first-stage regression of the 2SLS estimation
 procedure.
 The third column is the reduced-form regression, i.e., first-semester GPA
 is regressed on dummy variables indicating initial job placement and other
 individual covariates.
 It is important to compare the results of columns 2 and 3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 4 gives the instrumental variables estimates for first-semester GPA.
 This table provides evidence that the variation in first-semester GPA can
 be attributed to initial job assignment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Does Strenghtening Self-Defense Law Deter Crime or Escalate Violence? Evidence
 from Expansions to Castle Doctrine
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors perform a weighted OLS regression to account for the differences
 in population, as well as underlying state characteristics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Figure 1 shows the raw data.
 The authors are showing that the treatment and control groups track each
 other closely in the pre-period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Figure 2 provides the differences-in-differences estimates for different
 time periods.
 The authors compare the differences in the years specified by the 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

-axis relative to the difference 5 or more years before adoption.
 The divergence in the estimates should be relatively close to zero in the
 pre-period, while a signficant divergence occurs in the post-period following
 treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Figure 3 is a permutation based inference method, and it is one type of
 robustness test.
 The authors create data sets that are the same size as the true sample
 by randomly selecting 11 years from the pre-period.
 Then, they randomly assign 20 states into the treatment group and 13 states
 into the control group.
 Finally, arbitrary differences-in-differences estimates are obtained using
 these empirically constructed data sets.
 The vertical lines represent the actual estimates from the true sample,
 which can be found in Table 5.
 The acutal estimates should be as far to the right of the distribution
 as possible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 3 is an example of a falsification test.
 The castle doctrine law should have only affected serious crimes, such
 as violent crimes, homicides, robbery, etc.
 Yet, it should not have had any effect on non-violent or petty crimes,
 such as larceny and motor vehicle theft.
 Across the columns, the coefficients should relatively remain the same.
 Yet, column 7 of panel B has relatively large effects, which could be a
 problem.
 This is the ex-post motivation for why the authors control for region-by-year
 effects, as opposed to just-year effects.
 In this setup, the authors are assuming that time effects are the same
 within a given region.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 4 gives the deterrence effects of castle doctrine laws on burglary,
 robbery, and aggravated assault.
 If the castle doctrine is satisfying its original intent, then it should
 help deter the three crimes being evaluated.
 Thus, there should be negative coefficients for the treatment variable.
 Ideally, the authors are hoping for coefficients that are close to zero
 and insignificant for at least panel B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 5 gives the effect castle doctrine laws have on homicides.
 The authors do not want their estimates to change significantly as they
 control for additional fixed effects.
 It is important to note that the cluster robust variance-covariance estimates
 have poor finite sample properties because the standard errors are too
 small.
 Consequently, the standard errors may be too small for weighted least squares
 because it is limiting the effective finite sample, which causes the estimates
 in panel A to become statistically significant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Why does Table 5 show that there is an increase in homicides due to the
 castle doctrine laws? What kind of homicides are occuring? Is there an
 increase in the number of homicides in which innocent peole are being killed?
 Table 6 gives the effect of castle doctrine laws on murder, felony-type
 homicide, proportion of robberies committed using a gun, and justifiable
 homicide by private citizens.
 Panels B and C provide decent evidence that there was not a significant
 increase in homocides against innocent people.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Note: Standard errors can go up when we control for additional fixed effects
 because there is less variation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Does the Media Matter? A Field Experiment Measuring the Effect of News on
 Voting Behavior and Political Opinions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The study had some important limitations, most important of which was the
 relatively small sample size.
 Consequently, the standard errors of the estimated treatment effects were
 often large.
 The standard errors were such that the authors failed to reject (two-sided,
 at the five percent level) the null hypothesis of no effect if we they
 obtained a treatment effect estimate of less than a 3.6 percentage point
 change in voter turnout, a 7 percentage point increase in likelihood of
 voting for the Democratic candidate, or less than an 8.6 percentage point
 differential between the 
\emph on
Post
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
Times
\emph default
 groups in likelihood of voting for the democratic candidate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 1A gives the summary statistics from the baseline survey of all respondent
s.
 If treatment was randomly assigned, then there should not be a significant
 difference in the means of the variables listed in the table.
 So, the authors check to see if the means of the two treatment groups and
 one control group are equal to each other.
 Column 5 reports the p-values for chi-squared tests of independence between
 treatmens for each variable, and these p-values should be large.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 1B gives the summary statistics from the baseline survey of only those
 individuals who completed the follow-up survey.
 Important to note, information from the first survey is obtained after
 treatment is randomly assigned.
 Yet, attrition could be a problem because people may not respond to the
 follow-up survey.
 Even though there are incomplete response rates on the second survey, it
 essentially looks like the variation in treatment is random based on the
 dimensions we can observe.
 On a different note, the subjects should be unaware that the authors sent
 the papers in order to obtain honest responses when conducting the surverys.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 1C gives the summary statistics for the outcome measures using the
 raw data.
 Important to note, if there is attrition based on unobserved variables
 that are correlated with the outcome measures but not predicted by the
 observable, the results may be biased.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One limitation of the study is that while the authors know which households
 received newspapers, they cannot be sure that the newspapers were read.
 Ideally, all the people who received the 
\emph on
Post
\emph default
 or the 
\emph on
Times
\emph default
 also read the newspapers.
 Table 2 shows the relationship between treatment assignment and five distributi
on and readership outcomes.
 Now, in column 2 for the 
\emph on
Post
\emph default
 treatment and column 3 for the 
\emph on
Times
\emph default
 treatment, why are the estimates not 1? The authors people were misresponding
 to the survey question.
 Yet, a potential problem could be that the actual differences in treatment
 are very small, i.e., a weak instrumental variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 3 gives the effect of treatment on political knowledge and attitude.
 Table 4 gives the effect of treatment on voting behavior in Virginia governors
 race.
 From these two tables, one can determine that there is no statistically
 significant difference in the outcome variables between the two treatment
 groups and the control group.
 How percise are all of the presented estimates? It depends because there
 may be evidence of a weak first stage, and the variation in actual treatment
 may be very small.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Ruggedness: The Blessing of Bad Geography in Africa
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There is a positive correlation between ruggedness and income for many African
 countries, as opposed to a negative correlation for the rest of the countries
 of the world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Is the impact of rugged terrain different in Africa than everywhere else?
 The authors claim that the rugged terrain was good for Africa because it
 reduced the slave trade in many areas.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In order to answer the question above, the authors utilize a difference-in-diffe
rences approach, but with no time element.
 The treatment variable is a continuous measure for terrain ruggedness;
 it is not a binary variable as it is for a majority of difference-in-difference
s papers.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The treatment group is African countries, and the control group is the rest
 of the countries of the world.
 The paper analyzes the effect of going from rugged terrain to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

not so rugged
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 terrain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If the slave trade trade had never happened, then the relationship between
 ruggedness and income for African countries should be the same as the rest
 of the world.
 
\series bold
\emph on
The identifying assumption is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The identification strategy employed in the paper can also be considered
 and IV approach where the outcome variable is income per capita for a particula
r country, the endogenous variable is a binary variable indicating the presence
 of the slave trade, and the instrument is a continuous variable that measures
 the level of ruggedness in a region.
 
\series bold
\emph on
However, the authors do not take an explicit stance with this setup because
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Table 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors are worried about that the correlation between ruggedness and
 other characteristics is different in Africa than other parts of the world.
 Also, the authors do not want rugged areas in Africa to be better in some
 observable dimensions than the rest of the world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Columns 2 to 6 control for potentially confounding factors, which may have
 differential effects on outcome within and outside of Africa.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Table 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors do not possess a proper measure of ruggedness.
 Depending on how you manipulate the data, it can be skewed in different
 ways.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors perform a zero-skewness Box-Cox power transformation on the
 ruggedness variable to obtain a measure with zero skewness.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
With Table 2, the authors are essentially showing that different measures
 of ruggedness do not affect the estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Table 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A final source of possible concern is that the differential effect of ruggedness
 for Africa is not really an African effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Perhaps the differential effect arises because the effect of ruggedness
 on income differs in areas with some geographic characteristic that happens
 to be particularly relevant in Africa.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors consider the possibility that the indicator variable for Africa
 may be picking up the prevalence of colonial rule.
 In areas that were colonized, rugged terrain may have provided a way to
 defend against colonial rule.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Table 4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The slave trade was the most prominent in West Africa.
 The fact that there is a large, positive, and statistially significant
 effect for this region is good evidence of the identifying assumption.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Moreover, the slave was not very prevalent in North Africa.
 In the fourth column, we can see that there is a large, negative, and statistic
ally significant effect for this region, which follows the same pattern
 as the rest of the world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Do Better Schools Matter? Parental valuation of Elementary Education
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The author utilizes an identification strategy that is intuitively similar
 to the current regression discontinuity design, but the execution is somewhat
 different.
 The author essentially performs a cross-sectional analysis using very good
 controls.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If a river, highway, railroad, etc.
 is a boundary, then the houses close to these boundaries are removed from
 the observations.
 The reasons for this is that there may be a discontinuous and unobservable
 change in neighborhood quality at these natural boundaries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Boundary fixed effects is a good measure of neighborhood quality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Columns 1 and 5 of Table 2 show that the estimates do not change simply
 because of a change in sample size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 3 shows the differences in treatment.
 On either side of a boundary, there is a significant difference in test
 scores and housing prices.
 Yet, there should be no distinct differences in any other observable characteri
stics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 5 shows the results of a robustness test.
 The test would be more meaningful if the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 of column 4 increased by more.
 Based on the story told by the author, there should be bigger effects for
 larger houses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Labor Supply of Politicians
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The outcome variables were variables corresponding to the extensive and
 intensive margin of labor supply, as well as quality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors utilize a difference-in-differences research design.
 Instead of a binary treatment variable, the treatment variable is continuous.
 The paper compares the politicians who received big increase in salary
 (treatment group) with the politicians who received a small, or even a
 negative, change in salary (control group) as a result of the new measure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The identifying assumption is the following: in absence of treatment, the
 change in outcomes between the treatment and the control groups would have
 been the same.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If the identifying assumption is correct, then the estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{3}$
\end_inset

, seen in columns 2 to 4 of Table 3, should not change significantly as
 more controls are added to the regression equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This paper clusters standard errors on country because treatment occurs
 at the country level.
 There aren't very many clusters, so the authors may have to bootstrap to
 alleviate the problem.
\end_layout

